/-!
# Cyclotomic character values and reduction to the prime-to-p part

Source: the agent-written auxiliary ordinary-character argument in the DAG, motivated by
Illusie--Laszlo--Orgogozo, Travaux de Gabber, Exposé X, Proposition 1.2, printed p.138
(blueprint: lem-per-character-prime-part-reduction).
The blueprint ancestor is lem-per-artin-gabber-affine-curve, proof Step 3. Neither that
paragraph nor the cited proposition proves this auxiliary lemma. The requested reference,
Serre, Linear Representations of Finite Groups, Parts II (Chapter 10) and III, is unavailable.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

namespace Representation

/-- Ordinary complex character values lie in the integers of the specified cyclotomic field,
and their reduction in any field of characteristic p depends only on the prime-to-p part.

Source: agent-written auxiliary argument motivated by Travaux de Gabber, Exposé X,
Proposition 1.2, printed p.138, before equation (5), context only
(blueprint: lem-per-character-prime-part-reduction).

The function `χ` takes values in the actual integral closure of ℤ in ℚ(ζ). Its equation
in ℂ specifies every value uniquely as the trace, so it introduces no auxiliary choice of
character. `Representation` is a homomorphism to endomorphisms; because G is a group,
its values are invertible, exactly as for a homomorphism to the general linear group.

Proof (agent-written, as in the node):
1. For an abelian subgroup H, use `lem-per-finite-group-character-decomposition`
(Maschke and `IsSemisimpleModule.exists_linearEquiv_fin_dfinsupp`) to decompose V into
nonzero irreducible H-representations. Apply `lem-per-normal-commuting-weight-induction`
with normal subgroup H itself to each summand. Conjugation of characters is trivial,
so transitivity makes each summand a single character space. Finite spanning families
of the summands give bases (`def-weil-finite-dimensional-subspace`,
`def-weil-pbc-free-module`); their union gives a common eigenbasis. For V = 0 it is empty.
The field hypothesis is `lem-weil-complex-algebraically-closed`.
2. `lem-sfb-finite-additive-group-card-annihilator`, in multiplicative notation, gives
h^|G| = 1, hence h^N = 1. The N distinct powers of ζ exhaust the Nth roots of unity
by `lem-exc-field-roots-cyclic-bound`. These powers lie in ℚ(ζ)
(`def-weil-simple-field-extension`) and are integral over ℤ, as roots of T^N - 1.
Thus they lie in its integral closure (`def-weil-pbc-integral-closure`). Apply step 1 to
the cyclic subgroup generated by h. Every eigenvalue is an Nth root of unity, and
`def-weil-endomorphism-trace` gives the character as their sum, including the empty sum.
The integral closure is a subring, so this sum defines the specified value χ(h).
3. For any commuting s,u with g = su and u of p-power order, the subgroup they generate
is abelian. In the common eigenbasis of step 1, write α_i,β_i for their eigenvalues.
Step 2 places both in the integral closure, and β_i^(p^a) = 1 for some a ≥ 0.
The iterated Frobenius (`def-weil-ring-frobenius`) gives
(r(β_i) - 1)^(p^a) = r(β_i)^(p^a) - 1 = 0. A field has no nonzero nilpotent,
so r(β_i) = 1. The diagonal entries for su are α_i β_i; the trace formula and
ring-homomorphism laws yield r(χ(g)) = ∑ r(α_i)r(β_i) = ∑ r(α_i) = r(χ(s)).
This works for every qualifying commuting pair, whether or not they are powers of g.
4. Write |G| = p^a d with p not dividing d. Regard the cyclic subgroup generated by g
additively as a ℤ-module, annihilated by |G| by step 2. Apply
`lem-sc-coprime-torsion-decomposition` with the coprime integers d,p^a. The internal
direct sum (`def-weil-pbc-direct-sum-modules`) decomposes g as s+u with ds=0 and p^a u=0.
Multiplicatively, g=su; both lie in the cyclic subgroup and commute. Their orders divide
d and p^a, respectively, hence have the asserted forms. Their integer exponents may be
reduced modulo the positive |G| to express both as nonnegative powers of g.
This includes a=0, d=1, the trivial group, and the identity element. -/
theorem character_prime_part_reduction
    (N : ℕ) (hN : 1 ≤ N) (ζ : ℂ) (hζ : IsPrimitiveRoot ζ N)
    {G : Type u} [Group G] [Finite G] (hG : Nat.card G ∣ N)
    {V : Type v} [AddCommGroup V] [Module ℂ V] [FiniteDimensional ℂ V]
    (ρ : Representation ℂ G V) :
    ∃ χ : G → integralClosure ℤ (IntermediateField.adjoin ℚ ({ζ} : Set ℂ)),
      (∀ g : G, ((χ g).val : ℂ) = LinearMap.trace ℂ V (ρ g)) ∧
      (∀ (p : ℕ), p.Prime → ∀ (k : Type w) [Field k] [CharP k p]
        (r : integralClosure ℤ (IntermediateField.adjoin ℚ ({ζ} : Set ℂ)) →+* k)
        (g s u : G), g = s * u → Commute s u → Nat.Coprime (orderOf s) p →
        (∃ a : ℕ, orderOf u = p ^ a) → r (χ g) = r (χ s)) ∧
      (∀ (p : ℕ), p.Prime → ∀ g : G, ∃ s u : G,
        g = s * u ∧ Commute s u ∧ Nat.Coprime (orderOf s) p ∧
        (∃ a : ℕ, orderOf u = p ^ a) ∧
        (∃ m : ℕ, s = g ^ m) ∧ (∃ n : ℕ, u = g ^ n)) := by
  sorry

end Representation
